92
00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:16.000
Principal component analysis, or PCA, reduces the dimensionality of data,

93
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.000
in this case embeddings,

94
00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:21.000
and is often used to reduce the dimensionality of large data sets by transforming a

95
00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:25.000
large set of variables into a smaller one that still contains most of the

96
00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:27.000
information in the large set.

97
00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:31.000
A t-SNE plot, or a t-distributed stochastic neighbor embedding plot,

98
00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:34.000
is a type of data visualization used primarily to represent high-dimensional

99
00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:37.000
data in a two- or three-dimensional space.

100
00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:41.000
This technique is particularly useful for understanding complex data sets by

101
00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:42.000
revealing patterns, clusters,

102
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:45.000
and relationships between data points that might not be apparent in higher

103
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.000
dimensions.

104
00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:50.000
PCA is often used as a preliminary step to reduce the dimensionality of the

105
00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:51.000
data.

106
00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:55.000
This can be particularly important when working with very high-dimensional data

107
00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:59.000
as it helps to mitigate the curse of dimensionality before applying t-SNE.

108
00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:02.000
t-SNE can be computationally expensive and slow,

109
00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:03.000
especially with large data sets.

110
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:07.000
And so by using PCA to reduce the data to a lower dimensional space first,

111
00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:10.000
you get a significant speedup in the t-SNE process.

112
00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:11.000
For the t-SNE algorithm,

113
00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:14.000
perplexity is a very important hyperparameter that controls the number of

114
00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:17.000
neighbors that each point considers during dimensionality reduction.

